# Reflections — runnable proof-engineering demonstrations

This library collects the self-contained (core Lean, no `L4YAML` import) runnable
illustrations of the proof-engineering principles documented as numbered *Reflections* in
`Blueprint/08-initiative-4-intrinsic-foundations.md`.  They are kept separate from the
behavioural L4YAML test suites (parsing/emitting/round-trip) because they assert *methodology*,
not library behaviour: each is a toy model whose `#guard`/`#guard_msgs`/`#eval`/`by decide`
checks fail the build if the principle's positive/negative witnesses ever drift.

Each module names the Blueprint Reflection it illustrates:
* `ProducerGuardedTrap`                  — Reflection 218 (the producer-guarded-quantifier trap)
* `ConverseForwardAsymmetry`             — Reflection 219 (converse/forward invariant asymmetry)
* `ReductionByImport`                    — Reflection 225 (reduction by import — retype, not shrink)
* `ParametricAssemblerExtraction`        — Reflections 226 + 228 (parametric assembler extraction)
* `ConvergentReduction`                  — Reflection 227 (convergence corollary of reduction-by-import)
* `RecursiveDeliverableProjectToFlat`    — Reflections 234 + 235 (recursive deliverable, project-to-flat-first; single-level descent step)
* `ConsumerJointBeforeProducer`          — Reflection 231 (+ 232 / 237 / 241) (build the consumer joint before the producer; faithful mirror)
* `AdditiveParallelType`                 — Reflection 242 (land a forced deliverable refinement as an additive parallel type, never a shared edit; a dedicated type internalizes the guard)
* `UniversalPackagingJoint`              — Reflection 243 (universal packaging is its own consumer joint — the field/joint hypothesis gap IS the producer's deliverable type; build the assembler before the producer)
* `ProjectionFamilyCompletion`           — Reflection 244 (complete a newly-added deliverable type's projection family before the producer walks it — the omitted projection is the locate's navigation invariant, a verbatim mirror of the sibling's; projections track STORED fields, not desired properties)
* `EmitProducerStrengthening`            — Reflections 249 + 250 (the emit-producer strengthening is itself a consumer-joint-before-producer move at the emit boundary — key the assembler on a SUPERSET per-item predicate carrying the recursive deliverable (`RecEntry`, unrecoverable from the flat `FlatEntry`, witnessed by `flatentry_aa` + `not_recentry_aa`); and the recursive producer is a verbatim mirror of the flat one over ONE shared induction, only the leaf constructor swapped — `buildRecBody` is `buildFlatBody` with `RecBody.single/.cons` for `FlatBody.single/.cons`, `recbody_to_flatbody` projecting rec ⟹ flat. **R250 map layer**: the same strengthening mirrored across a SECOND axis (seq→map) — `RecPair` REUSES `RecEntry` verbatim for both key and value slots (predicate reuse), `buildRecMapBody` is `buildFlatMapBody` with the leaf swapped AND `buildRecBody` with the per-item predicate swapped (the two-axis mirror), and `recpair_len_floor` (`3 ≤ length`) is the load-bearing map-side scaffolding the seq body lacks — keep it, mirror the same-axis parent not the other-axis sibling)
* `ProducerDualOfConsumer`               — Reflections 245 + 246 + 247 + 248 (the producer's per-level assembler is the constructive dual of the consumer joint — same positional bridge, opposite direction, constructor vs. eliminator; its symmetric MIRROR transports the plumbing verbatim but sheds at the constructor exactly the field the additive parallel type projects instead of stores: seq `SEntry.seq` stores `WB`, map `MEntry.map` omits-and-projects it; the BUNDLE assembler `bundleLocatedSeq`/`bundleLocatedMap` lifts the field-level dual to the consumer's bundled deliverable `SLocated`/`MLocated` — only the recursive `entry` field is non-trivial, the rest are window-guard pass-throughs, and the map bundle threads one extra stored primitive `keyF` the entry producer never supplies; and R248 — that storage asymmetry is SCALE-FREE: the same projected-vs-stored mechanism SHRINKS the entry constructor and GROWS the bundle, opposite signs, witnessed by `wb_recovered` (post-hoc projection at the shrunk constructor) vs `mlocated_key` (the grown-in stored field projected back out))
* `FreeProjectionInvariant`              — Reflections 253 + 254 (a consumer-bundle field that is definitionally a CONJUNCT of a property the recursive deliverable already PROJECTS to is a FREE field, not a threaded obligation — read the deliverable's projection family before deciding what the recursion threads: `WB l := balance l = 0 ∧ ∀ i, 0 ≤ balance (l.take i)`, the recursive `Rec` projects to `WB` (`Rec.toWB`, `wrap` case = `WB_wrap`), so the bundle `Located`'s `dyck` field is `(Rec.toWB h).2` for free — `located_of_rec` takes only the `wt : WT` hypothesis and no `h_dyck`; the CONTRAST is `WT` (typed bracket matching), which `Rec` does NOT project to — `rec_mismatched : Rec [os,a,cm]` holds yet `not_wt_mismatched : ¬ WT [os,a,cm]`, so `dyck_mismatched_free` is still derivable but no `Located [os,a,cm]` is — the R244 stored-vs-projected split surfacing at the locate's invariant SET rather than a constructor's fields. **R254**: the one invariant with no local projection (`WT`) is recovered all the same — *non-locally*, from the OUTER window's `WT` via a subrange interface `WTsub` (the toy of `WellTyped_subrange`) whose two side conditions are the window's *free* balance + Dyck off `Rec.toWB`; the outer assembler `located_of_rec_outer` (toy of `seqLocated_of_recseqbody_outer`) threads ONE outer `WT`, recovers the window's `wt` through `WTsub`, and takes no per-window `WT` and no `dyck` — only the structural `Rec`; `wtsub_concrete`/`located_window_via_outer` witness it on `{ { a } } ⊃ { a }`, so Dyck is free LOCALLY and `WT` is free NON-LOCALLY, neither threaded per window)
* `StoredVsProjectedRecursionEdge`       — Reflections 251 + 252 (a recursive-deliverable inductive's STORAGE choices set the PRODUCER's recursion graph — `Entry.seq` stores the recursive `Body interior` so the producer's `seq` case recurses (`buildEntry` → `buildBody`), while `Entry.map` projects only the flat `bal interior = 0` so the producer's `map` case is a LEAF (`buildEntry` → `bal_emitList`, never `buildBody`), severing the recursion edge; witnessed by `mapLeaf` (a flat leaf builder for the projecting member, with no `seqLeaf` counterpart) and by `built_map_empty : Entry [om,cm]` (the projecting member accepts an interior with NO `Body`, even though `not_body_nil` shows `Body []` is uninhabited and the storing member therefore needs a dedicated `seqEmpty`); the producer targeting only projecting members is strictly smaller and dependency-light — a single self-contained induction — and in a producer family the projection severs the cross-producer edge, ordering the family. **R252**: the *dependent* member's IH is dead — `KeyEntry e := Entry e ∧ bal e = 0` (a saved-key-style twin deliverable) is built by the *non-recursive* `buildKeyEntry := ⟨buildEntry v, bal_emit v⟩`, which routes the recursive `Entry` part to the BASE sibling `buildEntry` and the extra flat fact to `bal_emit`, never calling itself — so the base carries the family's only live self-recursion and each dependent consumes it as a black box; `key_projects_to_entry` recovers the base `Entry` back out)
* `BoundaryBoundRecovery`                 — Reflections 255 + 256 (a consumer-joint-before-producer move has TWO boundaries — when the consumer quantifies a window UNBOUNDEDLY but the producer/assembler needs positional BOUNDS, hoist the bound-recovery into one joint that recovers the bounds ONCE from boundary invariants the enclosing context already owns, rather than re-proving them at every recursive call: `bound_lo` derives `2 ≤ lo` from the stream-start token `toks[0]! = SS` + the opener guard `toks[lo-1]! = OB` (the `OB ≠ SS` distinctness rules out `lo ≤ 1`, where `lo-1 = 0`), `bound_hi` derives `hi ≤ length-2` from `toks[length-1]! = SE`, and `locator_of_window_recbody` (toy of `seqLocator_of_window_recseqbody`) collapses the unbounded locator to the bounded per-window producer `h_rec`; the boundary token is load-bearing — `bad = [OB,A,CB]` with no `SS` frame satisfies the opener guard at `lo = 1` yet `¬ (2 ≤ 1)`, the window the missing boundary token would have excluded, witnessed by `neg_no_ss`. **R256 map mirror**: `bound_lo_map`/`bound_hi_map` are the verbatim twins over the map brackets `MB`/`ME`, reading the SAME `SS`/`SE` frame tokens (the recovery is bracket-agnostic — the stream frame does not know which collection a window opens); the R246 storage asymmetry surfaces as ONE extra per-window producer hypothesis `h_prim` (toy of the six pair primitives) on `locator_of_window_recmapbody`, which recovers the bounds ONCE and feeds them to BOTH `h_rec` and `h_prim` — `pos_lo_map`/`pos_hi_map`/`mapPrim goodMap 2 3` positive, `neg_no_ss_map` negative)
* `FoldConsumerChain`                     — Reflection 257 (fold a joint-by-joint consumer chain into ONE lemma keyed only on the producer's contract, before writing the producer — the fold costs nothing and cannot regress but converts a scattered residual into one typed boundary: `jBound` (boundary-anchoring, recovers `2 ≤ lo` from the frame token `toks[0]! = SS`) and `jPack` (universal packaging) compose into `targetOfProducer`, keyed only on the abstract producer `hProd` and the in-scope frame fact `hSS`; `located_via_fold` shows an arbitrary-window `Located` then follows from the producer + frame alone; positive `prod_good : Producer good 2 3 := by decide` on `good = [SS,OB,A,CB,SE]`, negative `neg_no_ss` on `bad = [OB,A,CB]` where the opener guard holds at `lo = 1` yet `2 ≤ 1` is false — the frame token is load-bearing for the fold)
* `UniversalProducerRootSeed`              — Reflections 258 + 259 (a universal producer's first brick is its ROOT SEED — the positional packaging of its one already-available instance, built as a faithful mirror of the flat-deliverable characterization with the payload swapped: once the consumer is folded (R257), the producer starts at the recursion's base case, not the recursion. `charRec` is the verbatim mirror of `charFlat` — same `subst`/`drop_pre` body, the flat payload `Flat` swapped for the recursive `Rec`; `root_instance` shows the seed IS the `lo = pre.length` (outer-window) instance of the universal `UnivRec`, the rest deferred to the navigation recursion (so `UnivRec` is left open). Positive `rec_good`/`seed_root` on `good = [OB,A,CB]` with a length-1 prefix recovered by `drop 1`; negative `not_rec_empty` on `empty = []` via the `Rec ⟹ Flat` projection — the recursive deliverable can never produce an empty block, so the seed never fires there. **R259 map extension**: the seed mirrors verbatim across the seq→map collection axis — `charMap` is the *verbatim* copy of `charRec` (payload `Rec` swapped for the parallel `RecMap`), the positional packaging step being collection-agnostic; the only deltas live in the *feed*, the R246 storage asymmetry one tier up at the seed's hypothesis count — `MapFeed` carries an extra `ska` precondition (toy of `simpleKeyAllowed`) and an extra `floor : 3 ≤ length` (toy of the R250 `3 ≤ n` map floor), BOTH absent on `SeqFeed`, and `seedMap` ignores them exactly as the real lemma ignores `_h_n3`. The floor is load-bearing: `seqfeed_singleton : SeqFeed [A]` is inhabited but `no_mapfeed_singleton : ¬ MapFeed [A]` (`3 ≤ 1` false), the seq side having no such floor; positive `seed_map_root` on `good`, negative `not_recmap_empty` via the `RecMap ⟹ Flat` projection)
* `DescendStepConsumerJoint`                — Reflections 260 + 261 (the navigation recursion's *descend step* IS a consumer joint's internal computation, truncated: returned as a value instead of consumed — and its symmetric map mirror. A consumer joint that peels/decomposes a located entry, descends one nesting level, then *consumes* the descended sub-deliverable into a terminal already contains the producer-side descend primitive its dual recursion needs — the recursion needs that sub-deliverable *as a value* for its IH, not the consumed terminal, so the descend step is the same proof body truncated before the consume step. Toy `RecEntry`/`RecBody` (mutual): `seq_interior` is the shared single-level descent; `recbody_of_entry` is the descend primitive returning `RecBody interior ∨ interior = []`; `flat_of_entry` is the consumer joint — defined literally as `(recbody_of_entry …).imp RecBody.toFlat id`, so the factoring is manifest: the joint = the descend primitive followed by the `toFlat` consume step, the primitive = the joint with that step truncated. Positive `descend_one` returns the recursable `RecBody [A]` while `consume_one` keeps only the projected `Flat [A]`; `descend_twice` descends the doubly-nested `[OB,OB,A,CB,CB]` two levels using only the primitive (the joint's `Flat` cannot be re-descended); negative `no_descend_scalar` — a scalar entry `[A]` has no `OB :: (… ++ [CB])` bracket shape, so the descent never fires. **R261 map mirror** (`## The map mirror` section): `MapEntry`/`MapBody` is the seq toy with NO scalar constructor — every entry is a bracket frame — so `map_interior` is *one case shorter* than `seq_interior` (no scalar to exclude), the toy reflection of the real map descend mirror's *dropped `h_op` opener-guard hypothesis*; `mapbody_of_entry`/`flat_of_map_entry`/`descend_map_one`/`descend_map_twice` are verbatim mirrors of the seq witnesses, and negative `no_mapentry_scalar` — a bare scalar `[A]` is not a `MapEntry` at all (the structural fact behind the dropped guard))
* `StructuralMovesAdvance`                  — Reflection 262, 263, 269 & 270 (the locate recursion's *structural moves* — descend + build + advance + terminate — one positional window lift per CONSTRUCTOR of the deliverable, base case included. A recursion producing a recursive-deliverable inductive needs exactly one positional move per *constructor* of the deliverable — not merely per way the constructors compose; for a body-of-entries deliverable (`single`/`cons`) that is DESCEND (into a nested entry's interior, eliminator side), BUILD (the same composition, constructor side — descend's dual), ADVANCE (chain entries via `RBody.cons`), and TERMINATE (the `single` base constructor). Once every constructor's lift lands — each a cheap verified brick — the recursion's residual collapses to the purely *analytical* entry-boundary location (the split points the moves consume). Subsidiary: the `RBody.cons` side-fields `h_ne`/`h_head` are free structural projections of the entry (`REntry.ne_nil`/`REntry.head_cstart`), not threaded obligations. Toy `REntry`/`RBody` (mutual) with a real `cons` body constructor: `recbody_of_entry` is DESCEND, `entry_of_recbody` is BUILD, `recbody_cons` is ADVANCE (discharging the two projections). The positives compose all moves on a *multi-entry* nested body — `body_AFA` builds `[A,FE,A]` by ADVANCE, `entry_AFA` wraps it by BUILD into `[OB,A,FE,A,CB]`, `descend_multi` DESCENDs back to the multi-entry interior, `body_full` ADVANCEs again; negatives `no_entry_empty`/`no_entry_separator` — an entry is never empty and never a bare separator, so the moves never misfire. **Extended by Reflection 263** with the *map mirror* of the advance step: a toy `MBody`/`MPair` (a body chains *pairs* `KY :: (block_k ++ VL :: block_v)` along `FE`), `mbody_cons` is the verbatim seq→map mirror of `recbody_cons` — only the terminal constructor (`MBody.cons` over an `MPair`) and the head side-field differ; the R246/R263 storage asymmetry surfaces as the *simpler* head projection `MPair.head_key` (one `cases`, single equality `= KY`, vs `REntry.head_cstart`'s four-case `CStart` disjunction); positives `pairAA`/`mbodyAA`/`mbody_two` (ADVANCE builds a two-pair body), negatives `no_mpair_empty`/`no_mpair_no_key` — a pair is never empty and its head is pinned to `KY`. **Extended by Reflection 269** with the *TERMINATE* move the original trio missed: `RBody` has two constructors (`single`/`cons`) but only `cons` had a lift, so the recursion had a step and no base; `recbody_single` lifts the `single` base constructor — the move ADVANCE defers to (its `h_rest` is dischargeable only at the last item). It is the cheapest move: `RBody.single` is indexed by the entry list *verbatim*, so it needs **no** positional plumbing (vs `recbody_cons`'s `e ++ FE :: rest`) and is axiom-clean `[propext]` — the complexity law that a move's cost = the gap between its constructor's index and the raw window, here nil. `body_AFA`/`body_AFAFA` now show the genuine recursion shape ADVANCE ∘ … ∘ TERMINATE; `mbody_single` is the verbatim map mirror, the R263 storage asymmetry recurring at the base move's head projection. **Extended by Reflection 270** (the map TERMINATE move's real-code landing, `recmapbody_single_window`): a base-case mirror is *free* — a mirror's cost is the plumbing it transports, and a base case has none, so the map terminate differs from the seq one only in the leaf constructor + projection family (axiom profile read off the original: both `[propext]`). `mbody_PFPFP` is the map-side recursion-shape witness — ADVANCE ∘ ADVANCE ∘ TERMINATE over a three-pair body, the structural twin of the seq `body_AFAFA`, identical move composition with `MBody`/`MPair`/`mbody_*` swapped for `RBody`/`REntry`/`recbody_*`)
* `LiftOnceMirrorEasyEdge`                  — Reflection 291 (lift once, then MIRROR the easy edge — the dividend-collection sequel to [[ref-easy-edge-guard-fails-hard-edge]] / R290 and the resolution step of [[ref-converse-forward-invariant-asymmetry]]. R290 diagnosed that a guard sized to its easy (ADVANCE) edge can be structurally incapable of its hard (DESCEND) edge, and lifted the content guard to the all-depth, balance-FREE `Deep`; R291 is the dividend: once lifted, the easy edge is ALREADY PAID FOR. A balance-free guard is a pure RESTRICTION of itself on every sub-window, and that property is direction-agnostic, so the advance edge restricts the same all-depth quantifiers to the tail `[m+1, hi)` exactly as the descend edge restricts them to the interior `[k+1, j)` — the ONLY difference is which field re-seats the child head (OPENER at `k` for descend, SEPARATOR at `m` for advance). POSITIVE: `deep_descend` (toy of `flowBodyContentDeep_descend`) and `deep_advance` (toy of `flowBodyContentDeep_advance`) have the IDENTICAL proof skeleton (`obtain ⟨_, h_op, h_fe⟩; refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_⟩`; head off one field; the two quantifier fields the parent's restricted by `omega`), differing only in the one head-seating line (`h_op k …` vs `h_fe m …`) — and `deep_advance` takes NO balance hypothesis and NO size side condition; `deep_descend_good` fires descend on `[ a a ]` → `[1,3)`, `deep_adv`/`deep_advance_good` fire advance on `a , a` → `[2,3)`, re-seating `hdAdv 2 = scal` with zero arithmetic (`#guard`ed). NEGATIVE: re-deriving advance from the depth-`0` PROJECTION `Shallow` costs the balance gate — on `[ , , ]` (`hdNeg = opn sep sep cls`, separators at depth `1`), `shallow_holds_neg` shows the shallow guard HOLDS vacuously (its gate `bal 0 k = 0` is false at the depth-`1` separators), yet `interior_after_sep_not_content` shows the post-separator head `hdNeg 2 = sep` is NOT content-start, so the shallow guard gives nothing toward advancing there; `not_deep_neg` confirms the deep guard's UN-gated separator field is exactly the repair (it rejects the stream). The lesson: after lifting a guard to carry its hard edge, do NOT re-derive the easy edge with the old projection-level machinery — write it as the hard edge's mirror, swapping only the head-seating field)
* `DescendStrengthInvariant`                — Reflection 290 (a guard sized to its easy (ADVANCE) edge can be structurally incapable of its hard (DESCEND) edge — the sequel to R289 and a fresh instance of [[ref-converse-forward-invariant-asymmetry]] fused with [[ref-probe-deferred-universal-before-producing]]. R289's content guard landed its ADVANCE edge cheaply, and that ease MASKED that the guard cannot carry the DESCEND edge: descending into a nested bracket `[k .. j]` must hand the interior head `hd (k+1)` as content-start, but that head sits at balance `1` while every field of the depth-`0` guard is gated by `bal lo k = 0` — no outer field even mentions the interior head. The edge is not hard to *prove*; the guard is too weak to *carry*. `Shallow f hd lo hi := isContentStart (hd lo) ∧ ∀ k, … hd k = .sep → bal f lo k = 0 → isContentStart (hd (k+1))` toys R289's `FlowBodyContent` (depth-`0`, no opener field); `Deep hd lo hi := isContentStart (hd lo) ∧ (∀ k, … tokDelta (hd k) = 1 → isContentStart (hd (k+1))) ∧ (∀ k, … hd k = .sep → isContentStart (hd (k+1)))` toys `FlowBodyContentDeep` — content at ALL depths, balance-FREE. POSITIVE: `deep_descend` (toy of `flowBodyContentDeep_descend`) transports `Deep` into the interior `[k+1, j)` as a pure RESTRICTION — child head off the parent's opener field at `k`, child fields the parent's quantifiers restricted, no re-basing/no bracket arithmetic; `deep_good`/`deep_descend_good` fire it on the nested `[ a a ]`, yielding the interior guard on `[1, 3)` with head `scal`. NEGATIVE: on the leading-separator interior `[ , a ]` (`hdBad = opn sep scal cls`), `shallow_holds` shows the shallow guard HOLDS (its only separator sits at balance `1`, so its depth-`0` gate `bal 0 1 = 0` is vacuous), yet `interior_head_not_contentstart` shows the interior head `hdBad 1 = sep` is NOT content-start — so the shallow guard, though true, gives nothing toward the interior guard; `not_deep` confirms the DEEP guard correctly REJECTS the same stream (its opener field at `0` demands exactly the interior head be content-start). The lesson: probe the hard edge's provability against the guard AS DEFINED — the remedy is a stronger invariant (lift to the descend edge's depth), not more proof effort)
* `AdditiveParallelGuard`                   — Reflection 289 (a too-weak shared guard's missing content is its own additive parallel guard — the guard-layer instance of [[ref-additive-parallel-type-over-shared-edit]]. The head-shape dispatch needs "head is content-start", and that is NOT derivable from the bracket guard `FlowBodyWindow` (balance/dyck/WellTyped): a separator-headed window is still balanced+dyck+WellTyped. `BracketWin f lo hi := bal f lo hi = 0 ∧ ∀ i, bal f lo i ≥ 0` toys the bracket guard; `dyck_excludes_closer` shows the dyck floor genuinely forbids a leading CLOSER (`tokDelta (hd lo) ≥ 0`), but `separator_head_passes_dyck` (`0 ≥ 0`) + `separator_head_not_contentstart` show a separator head clears the SAME floor while not being content-start, and `bracketwin_sep_head`/`sep_head_not_cs` exhibit a whole window passing the bracket guard whose head is a separator — so content-start is not a function of the guard. The fix: name the missing content as a parallel guard `ContentWin f hd lo hi := isContentStart (hd lo) ∧ ∀ k, … hd k = .sep → bal f lo k = 0 → isContentStart (hd (k+1))` (toy of `FlowBodyContent`'s `headContentStart` + `feContentStart`), defined as what the consumer already eats. POSITIVE: `content_advance` (toy of `flowBodyContent_advance`) transports `ContentWin` to the tail `[m+1, hi)` by pure re-basing — the tail head re-seated from `feContentStart` at the separator; `content_window_good`/`content_advance_good` fire it on `scal sep scal`, re-seating `hdGood 0 = scal` to `hdGood 2 = scal` across the separator at `1` (`#guard`ed). NEGATIVE: `not_contentwin_bad` — the empty-entry body `scal sep sep scal` is NOT a `ContentWin` (the separator at `1` is followed by another separator, not a content-start head), so `feContentStart` is the load-bearing field whose failure would break ADVANCE re-seating; the recursive DESCEND content edge — interior heads a nesting level down — is left as the isolated residual)
